**findElement() vs findElements()**

**findElement() - Returns ONE WebElement**
```java
WebElement element = driver.findElement(By.id("username"));
```
- Returns: **Single WebElement**
- If found: Returns the first matching element
- If NOT found: Throws **NoSuchElementException**
- Use when: You expect exactly one element

**findElements() - Returns LIST of WebElements**
```java
List<WebElement> elements = driver.findElements(By.className("item"));
```
- Returns: **List<WebElement>** (can be empty)
- If found: Returns all matching elements
- If NOT found: Returns **empty list** (no exception)
- Use when: You expect multiple elements or want to avoid exception

**Comparison Table:**

| Feature | findElement() | findElements() |
|---------|---------------|----------------|
| **Return Type** | WebElement | List<WebElement> |
| **Returns** | First match | All matches |
| **If No Match** | Exception | Empty list |
| **Use For** | Single element | Multiple elements |
| **Check Existence** | Try-catch | Check list size |

**Examples:**

**1. Single Element (findElement):**
```java
// Get one element
WebElement loginBtn = driver.findElement(By.id("login-btn"));
loginBtn.click();
```

**2. Multiple Elements (findElements):**
```java
// Get all links
List<WebElement> allLinks = driver.findElements(By.tagName("a"));
System.out.println("Total links: " + allLinks.size());

for (WebElement link : allLinks) {
    System.out.println(link.getText());
}
```

**3. Check if Element Exists (using findElements):**
```java
// ✅ Good - No exception thrown
List<WebElement> errors = driver.findElements(By.className("error"));
if (errors.size() > 0) {
    System.out.println("Error found: " + errors.get(0).getText());
} else {
    System.out.println("No errors");
}

// ❌ Bad - Exception if element doesn't exist
try {
    WebElement error = driver.findElement(By.className("error"));
    System.out.println("Error: " + error.getText());
} catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
    System.out.println("No errors");
}
```

**4. Count Elements:**
```java
// Count all buttons on page
int buttonCount = driver.findElements(By.tagName("button")).size();
System.out.println("Page has " + buttonCount + " buttons");
```

**5. Access Specific Element from List:**
```java
// Get all items and click the 3rd one
List<WebElement> items = driver.findElements(By.className("product"));
if (items.size() >= 3) {
    items.get(2).click(); // Index 2 = 3rd element (0-based)
}
```

**Common Patterns:**

```java
public class FindElementExamples {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();
        driver.get("https://example.com");

        // Single element - expected to exist
        WebElement searchBox = driver.findElement(By.name("q"));
        searchBox.sendKeys("Selenium");

        // Multiple elements - count them
        List<WebElement> results = driver.findElements(By.className("result"));
        System.out.println("Found " + results.size() + " results");

        // Check existence without exception
        List<WebElement> warnings = driver.findElements(By.className("warning"));
        if (!warnings.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Warning: " + warnings.get(0).getText());
        }

        // Get first element from list (safer)
        List<WebElement> buttons = driver.findElements(By.tagName("button"));
        if (!buttons.isEmpty()) {
            buttons.get(0).click();
        }

        driver.quit();
    }
}
```

**When to Use Each:**

**Use findElement():**
- You know element exists
- You want first match
- You want exception if element missing

**Use findElements():**
- Working with multiple elements (tables, lists, dropdowns)
- Checking if element exists (avoid exception)
- Counting elements
- Need to access specific element by index
- Safer option when element might not exist

